* R3d voltage-mode synchronous buck WITH soft-start (ngspice_augmentation,
* card #512, envelope-following round 10, 2026-09-29)
* run: ngspice -b env_r3d_softstart.cir
*   (writes to ~/Work/data/ngspice_augmentation/envelope/env_r3d_softstart/)
*
* Same power stage, compensator, dead-time network and PWM comparator as
* refs/r3d_vm_buck_deadtime.cir (see that file's own header for the full
* derivation of every component value) -- the ONLY change is Vrefsrc:
* instead of a fixed DC reference, it ramps 0 -> {vref} linearly over
* {tss}=3ms (1500 switching periods at fs=500kHz), so the loop's own
* v(vout) tracks a soft-start ramp instead of stepping straight to 3.3V.
* This is the target case for card #512's envelope-following work
* (tools/envelope_tran.py, docs/envelope.md): a transient that spans
* thousands of switching periods (2000 total here: 1500 during the ramp,
* 500 more settling after it) where a full plain `.tran` is the reference
* this document validates the envelope method against, not the thing being
* sped up in production use here (only the fixture; envelope_tran.py's own
* CLI runs the accelerated path separately).
*
* This file, run standalone as below, IS the ground-truth reference: a
* plain `.tran {tstep} {ttot} 0 uic` across the whole ramp plus settle,
* with the fine-resolution waveform written out for
* tools/envelope_tran.py's validation pass to compare against.
.param tss=3m ttot=4m
.param vin_v=12 fs=500k tclk={1/fs}
.param lval=10u rdcr=10m cval=100u esrval=10m rload=3.3
.param ra=2300 rb=1000 vref=1
.param r1=10k r2=27.51k r3=101.7 c1=36.35p c2=2.299n c3=6.261n
.param vramp=2
.param gm_oa=1000 ro_oa=100 co_oa=100p
.param gon=70 goff=1e-6 kc=100
.param rdt=430 rdisch=60 cdt=100p kswd=150
* kswd raised from a first trial's 15 (hand-estimated for a ~15ns
* transition, see the derivation above) to 150: found empirically (card
* #479) that gon=70 is large enough that even a small residual gate
* fraction (kswd=15's tail at, e.g., dly 0.19 away from the 0.5 threshold)
* still gives amp-scale conductance*V, so a genuine current-based overlap
* check (independently re-deriving I_hs/I_ls from the same B-source
* formulas ngspice itself evaluates, as tools/loss.py does) found up to
* ~0.78A of simultaneous hs/ls current with kswd=15 -- comparable to the
* ~1A load, failing the <1% bound outright, even though the two gate
* nodes' own 0.5-crossings were cleanly separated by ~20ns. kswd=150
* makes the tanh's tail fall off fast enough (conductance within ~1e-5 of
* goff by ~0.1 away from the 0.5 crossing, well inside the dead-time gap)
* that the residual current in the gap is negligible -- see MEASURED
* below and tests/test_refs_d.py's dead-time/overlap check. This does
* make the switch's own transition faster than the 10-20ns example the
* task gave (the RC network's own slew, times the now-much-narrower
* tanh window, works out to ~1-2ns -- still a genuine smooth/finite
* transition, just faster than the suggestion; a slower transition and a
* clean (<1% of load) dead-time gap turned out to be in tension for a
* single-stage tanh fed by this RC network's slew, and correctness (no
* shoot-through) was prioritised, per the task's own ranked pass/fail
* list).
* kc raised from R3's 30 to 100: found empirically (card #479) that the
* PWM comparator's own falling edge (which happens during the sawtooth
* ramp's SLOW rise portion, not its fast retrace) has an intrinsic width
* of ~1/kc/(ramp slew) -- at kc=30 that is ~32ns, comparable to (not much
* faster than) the dead-time network's own RC_on=43ns, so the dead-time
* network was NOT the dominant delay on that edge and the two switches'
* gate nodes crossed 0.5 at nearly the same instant (near-zero effective
* dead time on that edge, confirmed by a first trial: i(vin) showed a
* -210A shoot-through spike). kc=100 shrinks that intrinsic width to
* ~10ns, well under RC_on, so the RC network is what actually sets the
* delay on both edges. The OTHER edge (pwm's rising edge, at the ramp's
* fast retrace) was already fast at kc=30 and stays fast here.

Vin vin 0 DC {vin_v}

* smooth high-side (vin->sw) and low-side (sw->0) switches, driven by the
* dead-time-delayed gate nodes dly_hs/dly_ls (NOT directly by pwm/1-pwm,
* which is what R3 does and is exactly the missing-dead-time gap this deck
* fixes)
Bhs vin sw I = (V(vin)-V(sw)) * ({goff}+({gon}-{goff})*0.5*(1+tanh({kswd}*(V(dly_hs)-0.5))))
Bls sw 0   I = V(sw)              * ({goff}+({gon}-{goff})*0.5*(1+tanh({kswd}*(V(dly_ls)-0.5))))

* body/freewheel diode for the low side during the dead zone. Built as a
* smooth B-source (soft-knee: ~zero below vfbody, ~linear with an
* effective 1/gbody=20 mOhm "on-resistance" above it) rather than a native
* ngspice `D` device: found empirically (card #479) that tools/loss.py
* (used to check the 85%-efficiency/0.5%-energy-balance pass/fail bounds,
* per the task) has no handling for device kind `D` at all -- a native
* diode's dissipated power is silently excluded from its sum, and this
* device carries enough current during the dead-time interval (the
* inductor current freewheeling through it) that the resulting energy-
* balance residual came out to 0.5249% with a native D device, just over
* the 0.5% bound. loss.py DOES handle `B, I=expr` devices correctly (its
* own documented "bexpr" method, re-deriving current from the parsed
* expression -- see docs/loss.md), so rebuilding the body diode as one
* fixes the accounting without changing its physical behaviour: still a
* smooth, continuous function of Vd (no event, no ideal switch), just not
* ngspice's own exponential diode primitive. vfbody=0.6V, gbody=50 (1/
* gbody=20 mOhm, comparable to the switches' own Ron), kbody=50 (a
* deliberately soft ~20mV knee, since this is a control-style smooth
* model, not an attempt to match a real body diode's exponential I-V).
.param vfbody=0.6 gbody=50 kbody=50
Bbody 0 sw I = {gbody}*(-V(sw)-{vfbody})*0.5*(1+tanh({kbody}*(-V(sw)-{vfbody})))

Csw sw 0 100p
L1 sw swl {lval} IC=0
Rdcr swl vout {rdcr}
Resr vout cx {esrval}
Cout cx 0 {cval} IC=0
Rload vout 0 {rload}

* feedback: probe, divider, error amp (type-III, identical to R3), PWM comparator
.include ../../stb/lgprobe.lib
XP fbtop vout lgprobe
Ra fbtop fbnode {ra}
Rb fbnode 0 {rb}

Vrefsrc vrefnode 0 PWL(0 0 {tss} {vref} {ttot} {vref})
Goa 0 opout vrefnode compin {gm_oa}
Roa opout 0 {ro_oa}
Coa opout 0 {co_oa}

R1in fbnode compin {r1}
R3in fbnode n3 {r3}
C3in n3 compin {c3}
C1f compin opout {c1}
R2f compin n2 {r2}
C2f n2 opout {c2}

Vramp ramp 0 PULSE(0 {vramp} 0 {0.97*tclk} {0.03*tclk} 1n {tclk})
Bcmp pwm 0 V = 0.5*(1+tanh({kc}*(V(opout)-V(ramp))))

* dead-time steering network: pwm_inv is a separate B-source complement
* (not a wire tap) so it has the same zero-output-impedance drive as pwm
* itself and can source/sink the Rchg/Rdis branch currents cleanly
Bpwminv pwm_inv 0 V = 1-V(pwm)

* IS raised to 1e-6 (from a first trial's 1e-14, a "normal" ~0.6-0.7V-knee
* Si diode model): found empirically (card #479) that a normal diode knee
* is enormous relative to the 0-1V control-signal swing here, so the
* "fast discharge" path barely conducted for most of the transition and
* dly_hs/dly_ls ended up crossing 0.5 almost together anyway (a first
* trial's i(vin) showed a -159A spike). IS=1e-6 (Schottky-like, low knee)
* lets this small-signal steering diode actually turn on within the
* available ~1V swing.
.model ddt D(IS=1e-6 N=1.0)

Rchg_hs pwm dly_hs {rdt}
Ddis_hs dly_hs dis_hs ddt
Rdis_hs dis_hs pwm {rdisch}
Cdly_hs dly_hs 0 {cdt} IC=0

Rchg_ls pwm_inv dly_ls {rdt}
Ddis_ls dly_ls dis_ls ddt
Rdis_ls dis_ls pwm_inv {rdisch}
Cdly_ls dly_ls 0 {cdt} IC=0


.tran 2n {ttot} 0 uic
.control
set filetype=ascii
set wr_singlescale
set wr_vecnames
run
meas tran t_period TRIG v(pwm) VAL=0.5 RISE=1900 TARG v(pwm) VAL=0.5 RISE=1901
let fsw_meas = 1/t_period
meas tran vout_avg_early AVG v(vout) FROM=8u TO=10u
meas tran vout_avg_mid AVG v(vout) FROM=1500u TO=1502u
meas tran vout_avg_final AVG v(vout) FROM=3990u TO=4000u
meas tran vout_max_final MAX v(vout) FROM=3990u TO=4000u
meas tran vout_min_final MIN v(vout) FROM=3990u TO=4000u
let vout_ripple_final = vout_max_final - vout_min_final
print t_period fsw_meas vout_avg_early vout_avg_mid vout_avg_final vout_max_final vout_min_final vout_ripple_final
write ~/Work/data/ngspice_augmentation/envelope/env_r3d_softstart/env_r3d_softstart.raw
wrdata ~/Work/data/ngspice_augmentation/envelope/env_r3d_softstart/env_r3d_softstart_reference.txt v(vout) i(L1) v(vrefnode)
.endc
.end
